            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Key Takeaway: Account Tiering Is About Workflow Optimization</h2>
<p>Many teams implement account tiering by simply grouping accounts by platform or industry, then assigning management rules to each group. But this doesn't solve the core problem—you still end up monitoring every account.</p>
<p>The real purpose of account tiering is one: <strong>direct your team's energy where it matters most</strong>. In practice, this means classifying accounts by delivery stage, performance, and funding status, then allocating management frequency, approval processes, and resources accordingly.</p>
<p>One app development team's experience illustrates this well. They previously spent 2 hours daily checking all accounts, only to realize most of that time confirmed whether "normal accounts were still normal." After implementing tiered management, operators only needed to focus on core accounts, using automated rules and threshold alerts for the rest. Their daily monitoring time dropped from 2 hours to under 30 minutes.</p>
<h2>Three Dimensions That Determine Account Placement</h2>
<p>The tiering criteria are straightforward—focus on three dimensions:</p>
<p><strong>1. Delivery Stage</strong></p>
<p>New accounts in cold-start phases require frequent optimization due to high volatility and limited data samples. Stable accounts entering scaling or maintenance phases shift focus to creative testing and budget allocation. Declining accounts need assessment for optimization or pause decisions.</p>
<p><strong>2. Performance Status</strong></p>
<p>Don't evaluate solely on ROI—consider spend volume, conversion cost, and ROAS together. A high-spend, low-ROAS account demands more attention than a low-spend, low-ROAS account because the baseline impact is larger.</p>
<p><strong>3. Funding Status</strong></p>
<p>Accounts with frequent recharges and volatile balances risk service interruptions from funding issues—these warrant higher priority. Accounts with stable, long-cycle funding can operate at lower management frequency.</p>
<h2>Three Practical Principles to Avoid Pitfalls</h2>
<h3>Principle 1: Tiering Is Dynamic, Not a Fixed Classification</h3>
<p>Many teams establish their tiering at the start of the year and leave it unchanged until year-end. This defeats the purpose. Recommend reviewing and re-classifying accounts monthly based on the last 30 days of data.</p>
<h3>Principle 2: Don't Overcomplicate the Number of Tiers</h3>
<p>Some teams create 6 tiers and abandon the system within two days. Two to four tiers work best:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tier 1 (Priority Accounts)</strong>: High spend or high potential, requiring daily monitoring and rapid decision-making</li>
<li><strong>Tier 2 (Regular Accounts)</strong>: Stable delivery, weekly reviews sufficient</li>
<li><strong>Tier 3 (Observation Accounts)</strong>: New accounts or volatile performers, requiring intensive optimization</li>
<li><strong>Tier 4 (Dormant Accounts)</strong>: Paused or low-spend accounts, periodic status checks</li>
</ul>
<h3>Principle 3: Pair Each Tier with Clear Management Rules</h3>
<p>Without corresponding SOPs, tiering becomes meaningless. Define for each tier:</p>
<ul>
<li>Who is responsible</li>
<li>How frequently to check</li>
<li>What conditions trigger escalation</li>
<li>Whether automation rules are needed</li>
</ul>
<h2>Execution Roadmap: From Audit to Implementation</h2>
<p><strong>Step 1: Baseline Audit</strong></p>
<p>Pull 30-day data for all accounts—spend, conversions, conversion cost, and ROAS. Flag outliers.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Classify</strong></p>
<p>Cross-evaluate each account across the three dimensions. When dimensions conflict, prioritize funding status—funding interruptions have the most immediate impact.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Allocate Resources</strong></p>
<p>Assign senior operators to Tier 1 accounts with real-time alerts and fast-track approval channels. Mid-level operators handle Tier 2 accounts with weekly reviews. Tier 3 accounts go to junior staff or training candidates, with automatic escalation if optimization targets aren't met within 48 hours.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Establish Review Cadence</strong></p>
<p>Schedule weekly review sessions to examine all account data and assess whether tiering adjustments are needed. Track tiering accuracy as a metric, monitoring for accounts consistently misclassified.</p>
<hr />
<p>Account tiering isn't complex in theory, but execution requires discipline. The core principle is simple: <strong>let the right people review the right accounts, and reserve energy for those that genuinely need optimization</strong>. When done right, multi-account management becomes not a burden, but the infrastructure that supports stable scaling.</p>
